Dear Readers,&nbsp;

AI is finding cyber flaws swiftly. But are enterprises quick enough to fix them?

Nikesh Arora, Chairman and CEO of Palo Alto Networks, raises this crucial point. He notes in a LinkedIn post that most organisations, especially the smaller ones, do not have the capacity to  patch  flaws at the speed needed. Arora advocates virtual patching as an interim measure and calls for a shared vulnerability clearing house and faster development of protective signatures.&nbsp;

As AI agents become capable of handling everyday tasks, here’s a smart ring that promises to bring AI agents to our fingertips.&nbsp;

At a private launch in Bengaluru, Aina unveiled an AI-powered  ring  that combines a colour display, voice commands, and gesture-based controls. The startup wants its device to become an everyday interface between users and AI agents. But can it offer enough utility to justify its Rs 47,000 price tag?&nbsp;

In other news, the government has cleared 12 proposals worth over Rs 7,500 crore under the Electronics Components Manufacturing Scheme. This  round  of approvals adds to the momentum behind the scheme, which aims to build a domestic ecosystem for components and reduce India’s dependence on imports.

Meanwhile, MakeMyTrip has acquired a majority stake in Bengaluru-based Quick Ride, a tech-led shared mobility platform.  Quick Ride  connects people travelling along similar routes, enabling affordable ride pooling for everyday commute.&nbsp;

Lastly, here’s a bit of weekend motivation.&nbsp;

Built despite opposition, legal challenges, and questions over cost, Bihar Museum has marched into the global top 10, ahead of the Met and Louvre. For YourStory’s founder Shradha Sharma, the  museum  not only brings the region’s history to life, but is also a reminder of what ambition and perseverance can accomplish.

