LIU:  You were  interviewed by  The New York Times  in 2016 about CGC  (opens in new tab)  , and you said that making networked technology safer was a “challenge for civilization.” And, you know, what do you think about that? Has civilization made progress on, you know, resolving that challenge?   And what are some tangible takeaways that you think, you know, from 10 years ago till now, think through sort of how, how we’re making things safer for civilization?&nbsp;

WALKER:  I’d say that’s as true now as it was then, maybe more so. And I think our dependence on networked systems for every piece of civilization has only increased. I think it’s difficult to buy a car or to participate in a utility grid that isn’t networked, right.&nbsp;

How we’re doing? I think that we’re seeing a great disruption of the status quo right now. I think that we reached a pretty steady-state equilibrium, where our, our security in our networked civilization was, sort of, dictated by offensive scarcity. There was a known cost for high-end computer network attack, and that cost equation has been completely upended by the capabilities of models.&nbsp;

LIU:  So in thinking about, sort of, capabilities of models then, some terms that, that I’ve heard used are things when we’re testing models, for example, is––we call it–– red teaming . But then I also hear that there’s something called  blue teaming . And then, of course, if you’ve taken primary colors or understood kindergarten primary colors, if you put red and blue together, you get  purple teaming . Can you explain to the audience a little bit about those differences?&nbsp;

WALKER:  Yeah.  Red team  and  blue team  is sort of an artificial separation between being an attacker and being a defender, and it’s also, sort of, an oversimplification and an abnegation of capability, right. Attackers attack; defenders defend. But if you, if you take that simplification away and you look at what’s going on under the hood, you’ll see that the difference between offense and defense most of the time is, sort of, who’s in control or who’s driving.&nbsp;

If you were to give a name to a program that encrypted every file in your computer and then unlocked it when you needed it, that is either a cryptography utility or it’s ransomware depending on who holds the key. If you were to give a name to a program that, that allows you to log in to your computer remotely and control it, maybe your home automation setup, that is either malware or remote administration utility [LAUGHTER] depending on who holds the key, right? Who holds the key.&nbsp;

So there’s, there’s this, sort of, blurring of offense and defense where computer security capabilities at their most atomic basic level aren’t offensive or defensive. They’re dual use. And so the idea of  red teaming  and  blue teaming  is sort of this idea that we can impose single use onto dual-use capabilities, and  purple team  is the acknowledgment that that’s not true, right.&nbsp;

Attackers, as soon as they break into a network, they have an intrusion that they need to defend. You’ll hear all these terms like  moving laterally , which just means breaking into more computers, or  exfiltration , which means taking data. But the whole time they’re doing that, they’re trying not to get caught, right. So they are  defending  their attack, right.&nbsp;

LIU:  Mm.&nbsp;

WALKER:  And at the same time, when defenders are doing things like log analysis or really any—or traffic analysis—their tools themselves can be broken into, right. They have a duty to defend their own tooling, and they also need to, in order to prevent attackers, they need to be able to find vulnerabilities, find weaknesses, and then grade weaknesses in their own software. And the way that you find vulnerabilities is vulnerability research, and you grade weaknesses by writing exploits or at least proof of vulnerability, and that’s offense, right. So purple team is just this idea that attack and defense are the same skill set. They’re the same tooling. They’re blended. And it’s really a contest to control the logic of memory of networked computers run all the time.&nbsp;

